  "content": {
    "title": "If You Love ‘The Pitt,’ You’ll Love These Memoirs by Real E.R. Doctors",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTMemoirs from the front lines capture the high-octane pace, roller coaster stakes and unforgettable personalities of emergency medicine.Dr. Robby, Dr. Mohan and the rest of the Pittsburgh Trauma Medical Center staff navigate the emotional highs and lows of a day in the E.R. in the latest season of “The Pitt.”Credit...HBOApril 9, 2026There are as many memoirs about emergency medicine as there are sick people languishing in the waiting room in “The Pitt,” HBO’s gritty, gutsy medical drama. Here are a few that replicate the feel of watching the show — the high-octane pace, the roller coaster stakes, the unforgettable personalities — as Season 2 races toward its conclusion.ImageThe Blood of Strangersby Frank HuylerSuffused with the same intensity and abiding humanity as “The Pitt,” this slim collection of vignettes — by a physician and poet in Albuquerque — evokes the blood-soaked sights and smells of the emergency room.It opens like this: “We had no warning on the radio at all. The paramedics were urgent, moving quickly and breathing hard. Multiple gunshot wounds, they said, with unstable vital signs. … I took the young one. He lay soaked in sweat, with a blue-red hole in his neck. ‘I can’t move my feet,’ he yelled, over and over.”Between crises, Huyler pauses to witness. “It suddenly seemed very important that I look closely, as closely as I could,” he writes, “at this man taking on for the moment the role of the father to the dead son, kissing him softly, holding his hand.”ImageElisabeth Egan is a writer and editor at the Times Book Review. She has worked in the world of publishing for 30 years.Related ContentAdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
